+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+
+RFS policy with XDP and ordered CPUMAP
+====================================
+
+``samples/bpf/xdp_rfs`` demonstrates Receive Flow Steering as a BPF policy.
+It requires the optional ordered CPUMAP interfaces described in
+:doc:`map_cpumap`, XDP socket lookup, and XDP socket storage access.
+``CONFIG_RPS`` is not required. The policy does not modify the scheduler or
+add a kernel flow table.
+
+Policy
+------
+
+BPF tracing programs observe ``inet_sendmsg``, ``inet6_sendmsg``,
+``inet_recvmsg``, ``inet6_recvmsg``, ``inet_splice_eof`` and
+``tcp_splice_read`` at entry, and successful ``inet_accept`` at exit.
+When available, ``tcp_zerocopy_receive`` is also observed. The send observers
+use protocol entry points because ``inet_send_prepare`` can be inlined.
+They record the executing CPU in
+``BPF_MAP_TYPE_SK_STORAGE`` for established TCP and connected UDP sockets in
+the loader's network namespace. Error-queue receives are excluded. These
+are application-use observations, not the socket's incoming-packet CPU.
+They remain effective with ``CONFIG_RPS=n``.
+
+The hint is an aligned 32-bit CPU-plus-one value. Concurrent users of a
+socket have last-writer-wins semantics, like native RFS's advisory CPU hint.
+Hints persist until another observation or socket destruction; they do not
+have a timer. The sample does not observe SCTP, MPTCP subflow propagation or
+io_uring zero-copy receive. Ordinary sendmsg/recvmsg paths used by io_uring
+are observed when they reach the functions above. Missing optional tracing
+targets are reported by the loader.
+
+XDP hashes a directional address/port/protocol tuple into one of 4096 ordered
+domains. This assignment is independent of socket existence and remains
+unchanged for the map's lifetime, including during connection establishment,
+socket destruction, and socket storage allocation failures. Colliding flows
+share a migration barrier and can delay each other's CPU changes. There is
+no dynamic domain allocator, hash-map eviction, or domain reuse protocol.
+
+XDP looks up the socket, reads its hint without allocating socket storage,
+and releases the lookup reference. If the CPU belongs to the configured set,
+it becomes the desired CPU. Otherwise a stable tuple hash selects a CPU from
+that set. Lookup failures, missing storage and hints outside the set all
+take this fallback, still using the same ordered domain. Listeners and
+unconnected UDP sockets never provide hints.
+
+Every admitted packet uses ``bpf_cpumap_redirect()``. The kernel, rather than
+a racy BPF state query, decides when the old target has drained enough to
+permit migration. Queue exhaustion or target failure does not cause a local
+PASS fallback. The normal ordered CPUMAP drop and CPU-hotplug exceptions
+apply.
+
+Packet scope
+------------
+
+The example parses Ethernet with non-fragmented IPv4 TCP/UDP (including IPv4
+header options) or IPv6 TCP/UDP without extension headers. Other Ethernet
+protocols, VLAN encapsulation, fragments and IPv6 extension headers pass
+locally. Truncated headers needed for classification are dropped. The
+ordering contract therefore applies only to flows that consistently use
+the supported packet formats. In particular, mixing fragmented and
+non-fragmented packets of one flow can bypass the barrier. This example is
+not a fragmentation, tunneling or VLAN policy.
+
+Use it on a direct local unicast path. Socket lookup precedes netfilter and
+other demultiplexing decisions: NAT, VRF, reuseport BPF, sk_lookup policy,
+socket-map redirection, and VLAN devices in other namespaces require their
+own association policy. A successful early lookup is not proof that the
+stack will deliver the packet to that socket. Multicast and broadcast are
+outside the supported RFS policy.
+
+Control and lifetime
+--------------------
+
+The loader takes an interface and a comma-separated list of distinct online
+CPUs (at most 128). The CPU set and hash seed are immutable while loaded.
+It creates a queue of 2048 entries for each CPU before attaching ingress.
+It refuses to replace an existing XDP program. Driver XDP is the default;
+``-S`` selects generic XDP for drivers without native support.
+
+Keep the interface administratively down during initial attachment. Once
+the loader prints ``ready``, bring the interface up from another terminal.
+Before exiting, bring it down again and keep it down until the loader exits.
+SIGINT/SIGTERM while the interface is up leaves the program attached and
+waits for it to be brought down. The loader then queries all domains until
+source reservations and completion watermarks have drained, and detaches
+only its own ingress program. It leaves the interface down.
+
+The interface state is a control-plane contract: another controller must not
+bring it up during drain or inject test frames behind the stopped device.
+Changing the map, domain assignment or CPU set also requires this protocol.
+Disable native RPS and RFS on every RX queue; the loader checks both
+``rps_cpus`` and ``rps_flow_cnt`` at startup. A native RFS flow table can
+steer packets even with an empty RPS CPU mask. Downstream asynchronous
+steering is outside CPUMAP's completion boundary.
+
+Use a static CPU topology for the sample. Before CPU hotplug, stop ingress,
+drain and restart the sample with the intended online set. CPU online can
+require recreating a CPUMAP worker whose affinity was relaxed during CPU
+offline. The loader does not implement hotplug event tracking.
+
+On a drain error, or abrupt process death, the legacy XDP attachment retains
+its program and maps; the loader's tracing links disappear. Existing hints
+can therefore become stale, and new sockets use the hash fallback. This is
+not an automatic recovery service. Quiesce ingress before manual removal;
+do not replace the running program while its queues are outstanding.
+
+Building and running
+--------------------
+
+Use a kernel with BTF, BPF JIT, BPF events, function tracing, INET, NET_NS, and the
+ordered CPUMAP patchset. IPv6 observations additionally need IPv6 tracing
+targets. Build the samples as described in ``samples/bpf/README.rst``.
+To limit the existing samples Makefile to this program::
+
+  make M=samples/bpf tprogs-y=xdp_rfs always-y=xdp_rfs \
+       LINKED_SKELS=xdp_rfs.skel.h BPF_SRCS_LINKED=xdp_rfs.bpf.c
+
+For a separate kernel build/output directory, add ``O=/path/to/kernel-build``
+and ``MO=/path/to/sample-output``. Use ``CLANG``, ``LLC`` and ``BPFTOOL`` to
+select installed build tools when necessary.
+
+For example, in an isolated network namespace with native RPS disabled::
+
+  ip link set dev eth0 down
+  ./xdp_rfs -i eth0 -c 2,3
+
+After ``ready``, in a second terminal in the same namespace::
+
+  ip link set dev eth0 up
+  # Run the server, change its CPU affinity and generate client traffic.
+  ip link set dev eth0 down
+
+Then stop the loader with Ctrl-C. It prints per-CPU aggregated counters for
+hint/fallback selections, unsupported PASS, parser DROP, and observer storage
+allocation failures. Selection counts describe redirect requests, not
+successful ring admission or stack delivery. CPUMAP tracepoints expose its
+queue and redirect errors.
+
+Validation and cost
+-------------------
+
+``test_progs -t xdp_rfs`` runs the sample BPF source itself, with additional
+TC observation of stack delivery. It uses real TCP and connected UDP sockets,
+checks IPv4/IPv6 receive/send CPU changes, accept/splice/zero-copy observations,
+error-queue exclusion, missing/out-of-set hints, and socket recreation.
+Native live-frame tests check CPUMAP-to-UDP delivery and CPU migration;
+classification tests cover lookup misses and the unsupported-format boundary. Run
+``test_progs -t xdp_cpumap_ordered`` for migration barriers, concurrent
+producers, queue-full, entry replacement and hotplug infrastructure tests.
+
+The sample adds parsing, hashing, socket lookup, storage lookup, statistics
+and fentry work, as well as ordered CPUMAP's locks/accounting and GRO flush
+cost. Socket storage is allocated on first observation, not per XDP packet.
+No throughput or latency improvement is implied by functional tests.
+Measure these costs and domain collisions against native RFS and ordinary
+CPUMAP on the intended NIC and workload before deployment.
